M</given-names></name><name><surname>O&#x02019;Connell</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name><name><surname>Schmid</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name><name><surname>Perry</surname><given-names>CL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The validity of smoking self-reports by adolescents: a re-examination of the bogus pipeline procedure</article-title><source>Addict. Behav</source><year>1987</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>7</fpage><lpage>15</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3565116</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b6-ijerph-06-00463"><label>6.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hill</surname><given-names>AB</given-names></name></person-group><source>Principles of Medical Statistics</source><publisher-name>The Lancet Limited</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London, UK</publisher-loc><year>1961</year></citation></ref><ref id="b7-ijerph-06-00463"><label>7.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brittain</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A comparison of intent-to-treat and per-protocol results in antibiotic noninferiority trials</article-title><source>Stat. Med</source><year>2005</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15532089</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b8-ijerph-06-00463"><label>8.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>YJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Ellenberg</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Hirtz</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name><name><surname>Nelson</surname><given-names>KB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Analysis of clinical trials by treatment actually received: is it really an option?</article-title><source>Stat. Med</source><year>1991</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>1595</fpage><lpage>1605</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1947515</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b9-ijerph-06-00463"><label>9.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rosenbaum</surname><given-names>PR</given-names></name><name><surname>Rubin</surname><given-names>DB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The central role of the propensity score in observational studies for causal effects</article-title><source>Biometrika</source><year>1983</year><volume>70</volume><fpage>41</fpage><lpage>55</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b10-ijerph-06-00463"><label>10.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>D&#x02019;Agostino</surname><given-names>RB</given-names><suffix>Jr</suffix></name></person-group><article-title>Tutorial in Biostatistics: propensity score methods for bias reduction in the comparison of a treatment to a non-randomized control group</article-title><source>Stat. Med</source><year>1998</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>2265</fpage><lpage>2281</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9802183</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b11-ijerph-06-00463"><label>11.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bowden</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Turkington</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Instrumental variables</source><publisher-name>Cambridge University Press</publisher-name><publisher-loc>New York, USA</publisher-loc><year>1984</year></citation></ref><ref id="b12-ijerph-06-00463"><label>12.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Greene</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name></person-group><source>Econometric Analysis</source><publisher-name>Prentice Hall</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Upper Saddle River, New Jersey, USA</publisher-loc><year>1997</year></citation></ref><ref id="b13-ijerph-06-00463"><label>13.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Foster</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>IV for logistic regression: an illustration</article-title><source>Soc. Sci. Res</source><year>1997</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>487</fpage><lpage>504</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b14-ijerph-06-00463"><label>14.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hardin</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>sg61: Bivariate probit models</article-title><source>Stata Tech. Bull</source><year>1996</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>15</fpage><lpage>20</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b15-ijerph-06-00463"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rabe-Hesketh</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Skrondal</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Pickles</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><source>GLLAMM Manual. Working Paper 160. U.C. Berkeley Division of Biostatistics Working Paper Series</source><publisher-name>Berkeley Electronic Press</publisher-name><publisher-loc>Berkeley, CA, USA</publisher-loc><year>2004</year></citation></ref><ref id="b16-ijerph-06-00463"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brick</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalton</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Handling missing data in survey research</article-title><source>Stat. Method. Med. Res</source><year>1996</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>215</fpage><lpage>238</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b17-ijerph-06-00463"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author">